{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '90,180p;255,335p'; nl -ba test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc | sed -n '150,200p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '180,260p;405,450p'\"","aggregated_output":"    90\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n    91\tthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n    92\t\n    93\t```clojure\n    94\t;; request\n    95\t{:dao.stream/identity        id\n    96\t :dao.stream.remote/op       op        ; :dao.stream/descriptor |\n    97\t                                       ; :dao.stream/cursor |\n    98\t                                       ; :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    99\t :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n   100\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel\n   101\t                                       ; reader the asker holds\n   102\t :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n   103\t\n   104\t;; answer, the ordinary case: the contract's outcome map for the requested\n   105\t;; operation, verbatim, plus\n   106\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   107\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   108\t :dao.stream.remote/surface  S         ; descriptor answers only: the\n   109\t                                       ; entry's declared surface\n   110\t :dao.stream.remote/more     [...]}    ; optional, next only: further\n   111\t                                       ; outcome maps in order\n   112\t\n   113\t;; answer, a protocol error: one shape for every operation, a key instead of\n   114\t;; an outcome map, because no operation's outcome set can carry it\n   115\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   116\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   117\t :dao.stream.remote/error    e}        ; one of the three errors below\n   118\t```\n   119\t\n   120\tA request is one value on the channel writer; an answer is one value on the\n   121\tother end's. The request map is open: keys not named here pass to the mirror\n   122\tstep's middleware chain (section 7) and are otherwise ignored. A value that\n   123\tis neither a well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as a\n   124\tdiagnostic below the mirror step and the link, as malformed wire input\n   125\t(`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries). Wire ops are exactly four: `close!`\n   126\tnever crosses, because the mirror holds nothing per remote party to close\n   127\tand the owner's close is never remote; `attach!` never crosses, because it\n   128\tconsumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n   129\t\n   130\t**Protocol errors.** The mirror answers three errors with the error key,\n   131\tnever by fabricating an outcome the operation's closed set forbids\n   132\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention; Surfaces):\n   133\t\n   134\t- `not-found`: the identity is absent from the table, whether never served,\n   135\t  retired by policy, or reclaimed with its lease. An identity this peer does\n   136\t  not serve has no operations and no surface, so this one error is the\n   137\t  honest answer for all four ops.\n   138\t- `no-surface`: the op is outside the entry's declared surface, under the\n   139\t  fixed mapping: `cursor` and `next` need `:reader`, `append!` needs\n   140\t  `:writer`, `descriptor` needs no surface and is always answerable.\n   141\t- `oversize`: the channel writer refused the answer value itself\n   142\t  (`invalid-value`); the read is not skipped and no cursor advances past\n   143\t  it. Large media travel as `dao.jing` addresses (`dao.stream.md`,\n   144\t  Granularity).\n   145\t\n   146\tAll three names live under `:dao.stream.remote/`. The declared surface is\n   147\tanswer data, not descriptor data: the remote descriptor (2.2) carries no\n   148\tsurface, and the `descriptor` answer carries it under\n   149\t`:dao.stream.remote/surface`, the only place the surface is learned.\n   150\t\n   151\t### 2.2 The remote descriptor\n   152\t\n   153\t```clojure\n   154\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/remote\n   155\t :dao.stream/identity  id                      ; the served logical stream\n   156\t :dao.stream/channel   <channel descriptor>}   ; section 3: ws, udp or pair\n   157\t```\n   158\t\n   159\tThe descriptor names a stream and a channel. It carries no authorization, no\n   160\tsurface and no anchors; the mirror answers those. Two remote descriptors\n   161\tthat name one identity through different channels reach one stream, as the\n   162\tcontract's Envelopes section requires of descriptors for one logical stream\n   163\tserved by one transport (OD-3 (a), accepted). Attaching through this\n   164\ttransport never creates: a dynamic dispatch table has an\n   165\t`:dao.stream/attach` entry for `:dao.stream/remote` and no\n   166\t`:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   167\t\n   168\t### 2.3 The mirror step\n   169\t\n   170\t`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`. For each\n   171\trequest read from `chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n   172\t\n   173\t1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append the answer\n   174\t   with `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/not-found`.\n   175\t2. Present, but the op outside the entry's declared surface under 2.1's\n   176\t   mapping: append the answer with\n   177\t   `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`.\n   178\t3. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   179\t   directly, adding `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; and apply\n   180\t   `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and `:dao.stream/append!` to it\n   255\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   256\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   257\t\n   258\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   259\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   260\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   261\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   262\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A filed\n   263\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   264\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   265\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   266\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n   267\tthe channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n   268\tleaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n   269\tthe retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n   270\t\n   271\t**Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n   272\tprojection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n   273\tend (3.3), or the medium is gone. The link then abandons its outstanding\n   274\tids and reports each\n   275\tabandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n   276\tanswers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n   277\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n   278\t`append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n   279\tthe channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n   280\tcaller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n   281\t\n   282\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   283\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   284\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   285\t\n   286\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n   287\tdescriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n   288\tprobe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n   289\t`#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n   290\tlack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n   291\thandle-relative, as the contract's Concurrency section already makes every\n   292\toutcome: one perspective at one moment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never\n   293\tanswers `cursor-mismatch` or `invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only\n   294\trelayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n   295\tthe event writer.\n   296\t\n   297\t### 2.5 Loss and resend\n   298\t\n   299\tThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n   300\tmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n   301\twhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n   302\t`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n   303\tthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n   304\tor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n   305\tdeduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n   306\tappend whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n   307\tevent writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   308\t:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n   309\tThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no result\n   310\tretention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n   311\treads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n   312\t\n   313\t## 3. Channels\n   314\t\n   315\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   316\tis ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n   317\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n   318\tper channel.\n   319\t\n   320\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   321\t\n   322\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n   323\ta channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n   324\tattachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n   325\t`{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n   326\twired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n   327\t`{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value v}` message in the negotiated codec profile\n   328\t(Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile); the bounded acceptance\n   329\thandoff with slot and acknowledgement; the resolution and lifecycle\n   330\tvocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget unbounded.\n   331\t\n   332\t**New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n   333\tanswer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n   334\tthe ws deposit medium and the channel. The channel reader is a ring buffer\n   335\tthe composition wires; the channel writer is the ws handle itself.\n   150\t(defn- raw-fragment\n   151\t  \"One raw fragment envelope, `overrides` merged over a well-formed\n   152\t   two-part shape -- whatever a peer might put on the wire.\"\n   153\t  [overrides]\n   154\t  (merge {:dao.stream.remote/id 7\n   155\t          :dao.stream.udp/part 0\n   156\t          :dao.stream.udp/parts 2\n   157\t          :dao.stream.udp/direction :dao.stream.udp/request\n   158\t          :dao.stream.udp/bytes (cbor/encode \"x\")}\n   159\t         overrides))\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t(deftest malformed-fragments-drop-before-partial-state\n   163\t  (testing \"a fragment failing its own field checks is dropped: no\n   164\t            throw, no deposit, no partial state\"\n   165\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n   166\t          {:keys [send! log]} (spy)\n   167\t          sender (udp/make-port {:send! send! :traffic traffic})\n   168\t          receiver (udp/make-port\n   169\t                     {:send! (fn [& _] nil) :traffic traffic})\n   170\t          malformed [(raw-fragment {:dao.stream.udp/parts 0})\n   171\t                     (raw-fragment {:dao.stream.udp/part -1})\n   172\t                     (raw-fragment {:dao.stream.udp/part 2})\n   173\t                     (raw-fragment {:dao.stream.udp/part \"0\"})\n   174\t                     (raw-fragment {:dao.stream.udp/parts nil})\n   175\t                     (raw-fragment {:dao.stream.udp/direction\n   176\t                                    :dao.stream.udp/sideways})\n   177\t                     (raw-fragment {:dao.stream.udp/bytes \"not bytes\"})\n   178\t                     (dissoc (raw-fragment {})\n   179\t                              :dao.stream.udp/part)]\n   180\t          v (request \"s\" 7 (big-string 4000))\n   181\t          sent (do (udp/send-value! sender \"peer\" 9000 v)\n   182\t                   @log)]\n   183\t      (doseq [frag malformed]\n   184\t        (is (nil? (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 (cbor/encode frag)))\n   185\t            \"the malformed datagram is dropped, not thrown\"))\n   186\t      (is (= {} (:partial @receiver))\n   187\t          \"not one malformed fragment grew partial state\")\n   188\t      (is (= [] (deposited traffic)) \"and none deposited\")\n   189\t      (testing \"a real message interleaved with the malformed set\n   190\t                completes untouched by its neighbors\"\n   191\t        (doseq [[_ _ bytes] (butlast sent)]\n   192\t          (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 bytes))\n   193\t        (doseq [frag malformed]\n   194\t          (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 (cbor/encode frag)))\n   195\t        (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 (last (last sent)))\n   196\t        (is (= [v] (deposited traffic)))))))\n   197\t\n   198\t\n   199\t;; =============================================================================\n   200\t;; Loss of any part loses the message, recovered by resend\n   180\t(defn- fragments\n   181\t  \"Split encoded `payload` for message `id`, direction `direction`,\n   182\t   into fragment envelopes that each fit the datagram budget.\"\n   183\t  [payload id direction]\n   184\t  (let [total (blen payload)\n   185\t        parts (max 1 (ceil-div total (- max-datagram 16)))\n   186\t        size (or (chunk-size id parts direction)\n   187\t                 (throw (ex-info\n   188\t                          \"dao.stream.udp: no chunk size fits the budget\"\n   189\t                          {:id id :parts parts})))\n   190\t        parts (max parts (ceil-div total size))\n   191\t        size (or (chunk-size id parts direction) size)]\n   192\t    (for [i (range parts)\n   193\t          :let [start (* i size)\n   194\t                end (min total (+ start size))]]\n   195\t      (fragment-envelope id i parts direction (bslice payload start end)))))\n   196\t\n   197\t\n   198\t;; =============================================================================\n   199\t;; The port: shared local-socket protocol state\n   200\t;; =============================================================================\n   201\t\n   202\t(defn make-port\n   203\t  \"Compose one local socket's protocol state. `:send!` is the host\n   204\t   seam, `(send! host port bytes) -> nil`, called once per outbound\n   205\t   datagram; it must not wait or throw for an ordinary transport\n   206\t   failure. `:traffic` is the shared deposit target the composition\n   207\t   wires -- a writer every reassembled datagram's event is appended to\n   208\t   (`dao.stream/writer?`). `:max-message-bytes` and\n   209\t   `:max-partial-messages` are the two reassembly bounds (defaults\n   210\t   above).\"\n   211\t  [{:keys [send! traffic max-message-bytes max-partial-messages]}]\n   212\t  (when-not (and (fn? send!) (stream/writer? traffic))\n   213\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp port composition\"\n   214\t                    {:send! send! :traffic traffic})))\n   215\t  (atom {:send! send!\n   216\t         :traffic traffic\n   217\t         :max-message-bytes (or max-message-bytes\n   218\t                                default-max-message-bytes)\n   219\t         :max-partial-messages (or max-partial-messages\n   220\t                                   default-max-partial-messages)\n   221\t         :partial {}\n   222\t         :partial-order []}))\n   223\t\n   224\t\n   225\t(defn- send-datagram!\n   226\t  [port host dest-port bytes]\n   227\t  ((:send! @port) host dest-port bytes))\n   228\t\n   229\t\n   230\t(defn send-value!\n   231\t  \"Encode and send `v` toward `host`:`dest-port` from `port`'s socket:\n   232\t   a single datagram when it fits the budget, fragments when it does\n   233\t   not, transport-error naming oversize when the encoded message\n   234\t   exceeds :dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes -- never sent, torn or\n   235\t   partially sent.\"\n   236\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   237\t  (let [payload (cbor/encode v)\n   238\t        n (blen payload)]\n   239\t    (cond\n   240\t      (> n (:max-message-bytes @port))\n   241\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   242\t       :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}\n   243\t\n   244\t      (<= n max-datagram)\n   245\t      (do (send-datagram! port host dest-port payload)\n   246\t          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})\n   247\t\n   248\t      :else\n   249\t      (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)\n   250\t            direction (direction-of v)]\n   251\t        (doseq [frag (fragments payload id direction)]\n   252\t          (send-datagram! port host dest-port (cbor/encode frag)))\n   253\t        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))))\n   254\t\n   255\t\n   256\t(defn send-to!\n   257\t  \"The explicit-destination write path from an already-bound port,\n   258\t   outside any attach!'d descriptor -- hole punching depends on it\n   259\t   (3.2, section 4).\"\n   260\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   405\t;; =============================================================================\n   406\t\n   407\t(defn projection\n   408\t  \"Compose one projection over the attachment identity `:attachment`\n   409\t   (an `address-key`), the shared traffic reader `:traffic` this port\n   410\t   deposits onto, the already-minted reading cursor `:cursor`, and the\n   411\t   channel ring `:ring` this attachment's reflections and mirror steps\n   412\t   read. Owns the cursor from here: each `step!` advances it.\"\n   413\t  [{:keys [attachment traffic cursor ring]}]\n   414\t  (when-not (and (string? attachment)\n   415\t                 (stream/reader? traffic)\n   416\t                 (some? cursor)\n   417\t                 (stream/reader? ring)\n   418\t                 (stream/writer? ring))\n   419\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp projection composition\"\n   420\t                    {:attachment attachment :traffic traffic\n   421\t                     :cursor cursor :ring ring})))\n   422\t  (atom {:attachment attachment :traffic traffic :cursor cursor\n   423\t         :ring ring}))\n   424\t\n   425\t\n   426\t(defn step!\n   427\t  \"Drive the projection once, at the composition's cadence: read the\n   428\t   traffic medium to blocked, keeping this attachment's own events and\n   429\t   dropping every other attachment's, appending each kept value onto\n   430\t   the channel ring. A gap adopts the medium's own recovery cursor.\n   431\t   Returns the projection.\"\n   432\t  [project]\n   433\t  (loop []\n   434\t    (let [s @project\n   435\t          r (stream/next (:traffic s) (:cursor s))]\n   436\t      (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   437\t        :dao.stream/ok\n   438\t        (let [event (:dao.stream/value r)]\n   439\t          (swap! project assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   440\t          (when (= (:attachment s) (:dao.stream.udp/attachment event))\n   441\t            (stream/append! (:ring s) (:dao.stream.udp/value event)))\n   442\t          (recur))\n   443\t\n   444\t        :dao.stream/gap\n   445\t        (do (swap! project assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   446\t            (recur))\n   447\t\n   448\t        project))))\n   449\t\n   450\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
