{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '76,93p;350,386p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '720,765p'\"","aggregated_output":"    76\t  requests against its table. It holds no state between calls beyond the\n    77\t  channel reader's cursor.\n    78\t- **reflection**: the handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A\n    79\t  **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections on that channel\n    80\t  share.\n    81\t\n    82\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n    83\treflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\n    84\tkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\n    85\tend.\n    86\t\n    87\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    88\t\n    89\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n    90\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n    91\tthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n    92\t\n    93\t```clojure\n   350\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   351\t\n   352\t### 3.2 UDP\n   353\t\n   354\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   355\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   356\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   357\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   358\t\n   359\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   360\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   361\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   362\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   363\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   364\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   365\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   366\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   367\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   368\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   369\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   370\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   371\t  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n   372\t  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n   373\t  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n   374\t  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n   375\t  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n   376\t  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n   377\t  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n   378\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   379\t  in a payload.\n   380\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   381\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   382\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   383\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   384\t  republishes.\n   385\t\n   386\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   720\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   721\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   722\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   723\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   724\t\n   725\t### Complete history\n   726\t\n   727\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   728\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   729\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   730\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   731\t\n   732\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   733\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   734\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   735\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   736\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   737\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   738\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   739\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   740\t\n   741\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   742\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   743\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   744\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   745\t\n   746\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   747\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   748\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n   749\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n   750\tcursor is right and sufficient.\n   751\t\n   752\t**Wiring a log onto an evicting transport is a host assembly defect**, of the\n   753\tsame kind as wiring a deposit destination that can refuse. A durable log is not\n   754\ta window: if a consumer treats a stream as the record of everything that\n   755\thappened, the transport under it must declare that it retains everything.\n   756\tCapacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\n   757\tand never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\n   758\t\n   759\tThe obligation a complete-history transport takes on is one sentence: **a\n   760\tcomplete-history reader begins at its logical sequence's origin and never\n   761\tevicts an element of that sequence.** Every element present at creation, and\n   762\tevery value an append places on that same sequence with `ok`, remains part of\n   763\tit for as long as the logical stream exists. If the medium cannot serve\n   764\tretained history it reports `transport-error`; it never silently substitutes a\n   765\tsuffix. Everything else follows. Its `:oldest` anchor stays at the origin, so\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
