{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'transport-owned|Envelopes|namespace rule|namespaced' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -35; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '410,465p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '1,55p'\"","aggregated_output":"248:transport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n259:### Envelopes\n266:Every other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n288:  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n310:  transport-owned keys already permit.\n423:trails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n426:declaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n507:  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n698:Retention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n848:*Result Convention*, *Writing*, *Envelopes* and *Cursors*; the numbers are\n878:   the result map may carry, under a transport-owned key, a descriptor or\n882:2. State the end of existence: **destruction is transport-owned**, like any\n   410\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   411\t\n   412\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   413\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   414\tAn operation a handle does not\n   415\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   416\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   417\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   418\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   419\toperation exists.\n   420\t\n   421\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   422\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   423\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   424\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   425\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   426\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   427\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   428\t\n   429\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   430\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   431\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   432\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   433\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   434\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   435\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   436\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   437\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   438\t\n   439\tA handle may be composed over another handle on the same logical stream: it\n   440\tdeclares that stream's identity, implements no surface the inner handle\n   441\tlacks, passes cursors through unchanged, and may present values under its\n   442\town interpretation. Such a handle is host-composed, like a transport\n   443\tconstructor's closures, and is otherwise an ordinary handle.\n   444\t\n   445\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   446\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   447\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   448\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   449\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   450\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   451\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   452\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   453\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   454\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   455\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   456\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   457\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   458\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   459\t\n   460\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   461\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   462\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   463\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   464\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   465\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    34\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    35\t\n    36\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Streams\n    39\t\n    40\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    41\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    42\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    43\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    44\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    45\t\n    46\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    47\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    48\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    49\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    50\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    51\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    52\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    53\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    54\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    55\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
