{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '245,275p'; date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT'; TZ=Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z'\"","aggregated_output":"   245\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   246\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   247\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   248\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   249\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   250\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   251\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   252\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   253\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   254\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   255\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   256\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   257\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   258\t\n   259\t### Envelopes\n   260\t\n   261\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   262\t\n   263\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   264\t  dispatch key.\n   265\t\n   266\tEvery other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n   267\t(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n   268\t— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its\n   269\town keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.\n   270\t\n   271\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   272\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   273\tIts properties are settled:\n   274\t\n   275\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n2026-09-27 15:53:40 GMT\n2026-09-27 22:53:40 +07\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-27 15:53:40 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 22:53:40 +07\n\nThe round-1 P2 is fixed. Any of the four reserved fragment keys now routes a datagram through full validation, where a missing `:part` fails the integer check and is dropped before deposit or partial-state changes ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:268), [udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:289), [udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:391)). The regression test includes the missing-`:part` shape and checks for no deposit or partial state ([udp_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc:162)).\n\nA legitimate ordinary value inside a request’s `:dao.stream.remote/args` remains nested and is unaffected: fragment recognition examines only the decoded datagram’s top-level keys ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:93), [udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:273)). The `:dao.stream.udp/*` fragment fields are channel-internal wire fields ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:366)).\n\nI found no new defect in this fix. I could not rerun the focused tests because the read-only environment prevented Clojure from writing its classpath cache; the reported 12 tests, 36 assertions, 0 failures remains the available execution result.\n\nVerdict: READY\nSign-off: GRANTED"}}
